+## New Avenue
+
+**Bi-pencil `S_4` escape monodromy.** For a pinned `N=4` skeleton, restrict `F` to
+generic vertical and horizontal target lines. On a vertical line `P=c`, the
+Hamiltonian field `X_P` satisfies `X_P(Q)=1`; its incomplete trajectories escape
+at the finite set `A_F cap {P=c}`. Analytic continuation around those escape
+times gives a branch-cycle system in `S_4`. The horizontal pencil gives a second
+system on the same four sheets. The source curve `E=F^{-1}(A_F)` tells which
+escapes are shared and which components are etale over the cusp complement.
+
+This is not another boundary count. It is a global compatibility condition on
+one degree-four permutation representation, with the boundary skeleton supplying
+cycle lengths and pole orders as input. Single Hurwitz passports were too
+generous; the new constraint is that two pencils, the same four sheets, and the
+Hamiltonian translations must be compatible at once.
+
+Expected first target: the `D=105` skeleton above. If the coupled `S_4` CSP is
+empty, the smallest pinned counterexample target dies for a global reason. If it
+is nonempty, its output is a small list of monodromy skeletons to feed into the
+local Puiseux/Jacobian lift.
